''Ammonitidæ'', coiled in a plane spiral, and chambered within like the shell of the existing nautilus, to which the ammonites were allied. {{smaller|These shells have a nacreous lining and a porcelanous layer externally, and are smooth or rugose, the ridges straight, crooked, or undulated, and in some cases armed with projecting spines or tubercles. The species already described number about 500, and range from the Lias to the Chalk formations, inclusive. They vary in size from mere specks to 3 or 4 feet in diameter. Also written ''hammonite''. Sometimes called ''snakestone'', ''ammon-stone'', and formerly ''cornu Ammonis'' (Ammon’s horn).}}

